---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import ClassRef from '../../components/ClassRef.astro';
import Demo from '../../components/Demo.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
import ApiTable from '../../components/ApiTable.astro';
import DsaScore from '../../components/DsaScore.astro';
import { createRequire } from 'node:module';
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

/* The size argument below is computed from the shipped artifact — the
   hand-typed "10.3%" had drifted to a real 6.0% before anyone noticed
   (2026-08-21 sweep). Same projection, live numbers.

   The DENOMINATOR is derived from the same read as the numerator, and that
   is the whole point of this block. It was a hand-typed `12` while this
   stylesheet grew to 26 glyphs (137.1's toolbar set on 2026-08-24, then
   row-edit's two on 2026-08-27), so the projection divided 26 glyphs' worth
   of bytes by 12 and published a 200-icon set at 148 kB against a true 68.
   One read, so the two cannot drift apart again. */
const require = createRequire(import.meta.url);
const iconCss = readFileSync(require.resolve('@busy-office/ui/css/components/icon.min'), 'utf8');
const iconBytes = iconCss.length;
const totalBytes = readFileSync(require.resolve('@busy-office/ui/css/min'), 'utf8').length;
const iconShare = ((iconBytes / totalBytes) * 100).toFixed(1);
const glyphCount = new Set(iconCss.match(/\.bo-icon--[a-z0-9-]+/g) ?? []).size;
const totalKb = Math.round(totalBytes / 1024);
const catalogueKb = Math.round((iconBytes / glyphCount) * 200 / 1024);

const glyphs = ['doc', 'invoice', 'cart', 'check-circle', 'truck', 'box', 'chart', 'grid'];
/* DEPRECATED (roadmap 53.2): zero pattern screens in these docs render these —
   only this page's own showcase did. Kept for the deprecation window (next
   major), shown separately so the showcase stops implying they earned their
   place the way the eight above did. */
const deprecatedGlyphs = ['settings', 'barcode', 'building', 'user'];

const markup = `<!-- Decorative (label carries the meaning): -->
<a class="bo-sidebar-nav__link" href="/invoices">
  <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--invoice" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <span class="bo-sidebar-nav__label">Invoices</span>
</a>

<!-- Icon-only control labels ITSELF, never the icon: -->
<button class="bo-btn bo-btn--ghost bo-btn--icon" aria-label="Settings">
  <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--settings" aria-hidden="true"></span>
</button>

<!-- Add your own glyph in one line (any single-color SVG works): -->
<style>
  .my-icon--rocket { mask-image: url("data:image/svg+xml,<svg …></svg>"); }
</style>`;
const customIcon = `<span class="bo-icon" aria-hidden="true"
  style="--bo-icon-src: url('data:image/svg+xml,<svg xmlns=&quot;http://www.w3.org/2000/svg&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 24 24&quot; fill=&quot;none&quot; stroke=&quot;%23000&quot; stroke-width=&quot;2&quot; stroke-linecap=&quot;round&quot;><path d=&quot;M12 5v14M5 12h14&quot;/></svg>')"></span>
<span>Add line</span>`;
---
<Gallery title="Icon">
  <p class="demo-note">
    CSS-rendered icons: an inline-SVG <code>mask-image</code> painted by
    <code>currentColor</code> — real, <strong>themable</strong> glyphs with zero
    JS, zero font files, no network requests. Sized <code>1em</code>, so they track
    the surrounding font size (and density) automatically. The set is deliberately
    small — enough ERP glyphs to prove the mechanism, never a catalogue; adding
    your own is one line of CSS — or paste inline SVG with
    <code>fill="currentColor"</code>, which is an equally first-class answer.
    <strong>Not as the only label for a meaning the reader has to
    learn</strong> — an icon-only control works when the glyph is already a
    convention (close, search, settings) and space is genuinely tight, and
    then it still needs an <code>aria-label</code>, since these glyphs are
    <code>aria-hidden</code> decoration. It fails the moment the meaning is
    domain-specific: nobody recognises a mark for "post goods issue", so
    that button needs its words. And where no glyph in any set reads as the
    thing — "accounts payable" has none — use letters: an initials
    <a href={base + '/components/badge'}>badge</a> is honest where an
    approximate icon is confidently wrong.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The set</h2>
    <div class="bo-cluster" style="--bo-cluster-gap: var(--bo-space-4); font-size: 1.5rem">
      {glyphs.map((g) => (
        <span style="display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: var(--bo-space-1)">
          <span class={`bo-icon bo-icon--${g}`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <code style="font-size: var(--bo-font-size-xs)">--{g}</code>
        </span>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Convenience presets for ERP concepts common enough
    to be worth shipping a name for — each is just a value of
    <code>--bo-icon-src</code>, same as your own would be (next section).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Deprecated <span class="bo-badge bo-badge--warning">{deprecatedGlyphs.length}</span></h2>
    <div class="bo-cluster bo-u-text-muted" style="--bo-cluster-gap: var(--bo-space-4); font-size: 1.5rem">
      {deprecatedGlyphs.map((g) => (
        <span style="display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: var(--bo-space-1)">
          <span class={`bo-icon bo-icon--${g}`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <code style="font-size: var(--bo-font-size-xs)">--{g}</code>
        </span>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">No pattern screen in these docs renders any of
    these — only this showcase did. Kept shipping (removing published
    classes is a next-major change, not a tidy-up) but no longer recommended:
    point <code>--bo-icon-src</code> at your own SVG instead (next section).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Themable — it's just <code>currentColor</code></h2>
    <p style="font-size: 1.5rem">
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--check-circle" aria-hidden="true" style="color: var(--bo-color-success-text)"></span>
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--truck" aria-hidden="true" style="color: var(--bo-color-accent)"></span>
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--box" aria-hidden="true" style="color: var(--bo-color-danger-text)"></span>
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--chart" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Set <code>color</code> (or inherit it) and the glyph
    follows — semantic tokens, dark mode, and brand presets all work with no
    icon-specific wiring. Under forced-colors the icons repaint in the system text
    color instead of vanishing (mask icons need an explicit opt-out; shipped).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Sizes — font-size IS the size</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--invoice" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--invoice" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size: 1.5rem"></span>
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--invoice" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size: 2rem"></span>
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--invoice" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size: 3rem"></span>
    </p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">No size modifiers: <code>1em</code> means an icon in
    a compact table row is smaller than one in a spacious header for free.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>In context — sidebar links and icon buttons</h2>
    <nav class="bo-sidebar-nav demo-frame" aria-label="Icon demo" style="max-inline-size: 14rem">
      <ul>
        <li><a class="bo-sidebar-nav__link" href="#" aria-current="page"><span class="bo-icon bo-icon--invoice bo-sidebar-nav__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="bo-sidebar-nav__label">Invoices</span></a></li>
        <li><a class="bo-sidebar-nav__link" href="#"><span class="bo-icon bo-icon--check-circle bo-sidebar-nav__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="bo-sidebar-nav__label">Approvals</span></a></li>
        <li><a class="bo-sidebar-nav__link" href="#"><span class="bo-icon bo-icon--truck bo-sidebar-nav__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="bo-sidebar-nav__label">Shipments</span></a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <p style="margin-block-start: var(--bo-space-3)">
      <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--ghost bo-btn--icon" aria-label="Settings"><span class="bo-icon bo-icon--settings" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
      <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--ghost bo-btn--icon" aria-label="Reports"><span class="bo-icon bo-icon--chart" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
    </p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Composes with the existing
    <code>.bo-sidebar-nav__icon</code> slot and <code>.bo-btn--icon</code> —
    decorative icons carry <code>aria-hidden</code>; an icon-only button labels
    <em>itself</em> with <code>aria-label</code>, never the icon.
    Both buttons here pass the opener's test deliberately: a cog for
    settings and a bar chart for reports are conventions a reader already
    holds. The same treatment on a domain action — "post goods issue" —
    would not be, and that button gets its words.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Any icon you like — one custom property</h2>
    <p>The glyphs above are not a privileged list: each one is just a value of
    <code>--bo-icon-src</code>. Point that property at any SVG and the element gets the
    framework's sizing, <code>currentColor</code> painting, density tracking and
    forced-colors handling with <strong>no new class from us</strong>.</p>
    <Demo code={customIcon} />
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">That works with <a href="https://lucide.dev">Lucide</a>,
    <a href="https://heroicons.com">Heroicons</a> or your own brand mark — copy the SVG,
    percent-encode <code>#</code> as <code>%23</code>, and set the property. Icons drawn
    with <code>stroke="currentColor"</code> need no edit at all, because the mask ignores
    the SVG's own colours and paints with yours.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">
      <strong>Why there is no 200-icon catalogue here</strong>, since that is the obvious
      thing to ask for: the <strong>{glyphCount}</strong> glyphs shipped today are
      <strong>{iconShare}% of the whole framework</strong> by size, because every glyph is
      an inlined SVG. At that rate two hundred would add roughly
      <strong>{catalogueKb}&nbsp;kB</strong>, against a whole framework that is
      <strong>{totalKb}&nbsp;kB</strong> — to compete with icon sets that are better
      maintained and free. One property is the smaller answer and the more general one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Your own artwork — which mechanism, and when none</h2>
    <p>Four questions come up as "can we have SVG for this?". Three already have an
    answer that ships, and the fourth is a no.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">You want</th><th scope="col">Use</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">A one-colour glyph</th>
            <td><code>--bo-icon-src</code> on <code>.bo-icon</code></td>
            <td>It is a <strong>mask</strong>, so it takes your text colour and follows the
            theme, density and forced-colours handling automatically</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">A multi-colour mark — a logo, a vendor identicon</th>
            <td>a plain <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>, inside <a href={base + '/components/avatar'}>the avatar</a> if it is a person or an org</td>
            <td><strong>A mask cannot do this.</strong> It discards the artwork's own colours
            by design — that is what makes it themable. Multi-colour means an image</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">A per-tenant app tile</th>
            <td>the app launcher's inline <code>svg</code> slot</td>
            <td>Already the extension point: an inline <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> inherits
            <code>currentColor</code> exactly like the shipped glyphs</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">An initials avatar</th>
            <td><strong>no SVG at all</strong> — <a href={base + '/components/avatar'}><code>.bo-avatar</code></a> with two letters in it</td>
            <td>Text scales, translates, is selectable and needs no asset pipeline.
            Generating an SVG for it would be strictly worse</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">
      <strong>What this framework will not ship: an illustration set.</strong> Empty
      states and error screens are the places an ERP feels most broken, and the fix is
      almost always a clear sentence and the action that resolves it — not a drawing.
      A picture that has to be redrawn for every tenant's brand, translated for nothing,
      and downloaded on a warehouse tablet is cost without an answer. If you want one,
      it is an <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>, and it is <code>alt=""</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

    <h2>Markup</h2>
    <pre><code>{markup}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <ClassRef component="icon" />
  <ApiTable
    component="icon"
    notes={[
      'Painted by <code>currentColor</code> via <code>mask-image</code> — themes, dark mode, and brand presets apply with zero icon-specific wiring; no new contrast pairs needed (an icon inherits the already-gated color of its text context).',
      'Decorative icons (next to a visible label) are <code>aria-hidden="true"</code>; icon-only controls label THEMSELVES (<code>aria-label</code> on the button/link).',
      'Deliberately a small set, not a library — add your own with one <code>mask-image</code> line, or paste inline SVG with <code>fill="currentColor"</code>; both are first-class.',
      'Forced-colors: mask icons vanish by default (the paint is a background-color, which forced colors override) — the shipped opt-out repaints them in <code>CanvasText</code>.',
    ]}
  />
  <DsaScore component="icon" />
  <Related links={[["/components/sidebar-nav", "Sidebar navigation"], ["/patterns/app-launch", "App launch pattern"], ["/concepts/accessibility", "Accessibility model"]]} />
</Gallery>
